  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000119681,
      "SummaryText": "Hanwha Solutions Qcells (Qcells) endeavors in all its activities to promote a quality environment. Qcells recognizes that our activities may impact our employees and the surrounding community. Because of this, Qcells is dedicated to environmental protection, employee health and safety, regulatory compliance and pollution prevention through a strategy of continual improvement and teamwork.  \n\nOur corporate environmental, health, and safety (EHS) policy and requirements promote the overall safety of this facility, employees, and the public. This policy includes risk reduction programs that provide assurance for the safe management of process risks to prevent environmental, health and safety incidents and to protect the workplace, the community and the environment. These programs enable the plant to prevent the occurrence or minimize the consequences of significant releases of hazardous chemicals.\n\nIt is Qcells\u0092 policy to conduct our business in accordance with all applicable laws, regulations, and permits and to promote a safe workplace and a quality environment. Each employee is trained and required to comply strictly with this policy. We do not allow production goals or financial objectives to over-ride safety and environmental concerns.\n\n\nStationary Source and the Regulated Substances Handled\n\nThe Qcells facility in White, GA manufacturers solar panels for a variety of applications, including residential, commercial and industrial and utility. Anhydrous ammonia (ammonia) and silane are two chemicals used in the PERC solar cell panels fabrication process. Both ammonia and silane are purchased from and supplied to the facility by outside sources. The ammonia is delivered via ISO containers, and the silane is delivered via tube trailers. The ISO containers and tube trailers are used as the storage volume for the respective processes. \n\nThe storage containers are connected to the process piping in the bulk specialty gas systems (BSGS). In the BSGS, the ammonia and sila"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000119681,
      "SummaryText": "ne are heated to ensure it is vaporized and transferred to the gas isolation box (GIB) using process pressure. The GIB distributes the ammonia to four valve manifold boxes (VMBs), where the ammonia and silane supplies are divided further: six points of use for each VMB. \n\nThe ammonia and silane are used in the Rear Passivated Chemical Vapor Deposition (RPCVD) and the Anti Reflection Chemical Vapor Disposition (ARCVD) processes.\n\nThe maximum intended inventory for the ammonia process is 51000 lbs., and the maximum intended inventory for the silane process is 27000 lbs. \n\n\nGeneral Accidental Release Prevention Program and Chemical-Specific Prevention Steps\n\nThe facility\u0092s overall approach and commitment to chemical safety is demonstrated by 1) the management team, training, operating procedures and maintenance systems in place at the facility, and 2) the hazard assessment, prevention program and emergency response provisions implemented specifically for the hazardous chemicals stored and used onsite as part of the RMP requirements.\n\nQcells has implemented Program 3 Prevention Programs for the ammonia and silane processes. The prevention program provisions are specifically intended to minimize likelihood and/or consequences of any accidental releases from these processes. The prevention programs are ongoing systems based on process hazard reviews, procedure revisions, periodic training, inspection and testing programs, incident investigations, emergency planning and response, audits, and RMP revisions and updates as required to reflect facility changes and RMP requirements.\n\n\nFive-Year Accident History\n\nThere have been no accidents involving ammonia or silane in the last five years as this is a new construction manufacturing facility.\n\n\nEmergency Response Program\n\nThe Qcells facility intends to be a responding facility and has developed and implemented written Emergency Response Program in compliance with the requirements of 29 CFR 1910.120. The Emergency Response Prog"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 2,
      "FacilityID": 1000119681,
      "SummaryText": "ram is focused on the internal HAZWOPER trained team to handle accidental spills of ammonia and silane. Release events that are too large to be handled by the internal team are to be handled by an outside emergency response and clean up contractor. Qcells has made a significant effort to communicate the elements of its response program with local emergency responders. Members of the local fire department and Local Emergency Planning Committee have visited the facility. Facility personnel involved with the ammonia and silane processes receive regular training on pollution prevention and emergency response. This training includes participating in an emergency drill which is documented. Alert systems are also in place at the facility. As previously described the facility is equipped with ammonia and silane air monitoring devices to provide early warning of a release of either chemical.\n\n\nPlanned Changes to Improve Safety\n\nQcells continues to review its programs and procedures and make improvements where necessary. At this time, no significant improvement needs have been identified."
    }
  ]
